{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts using an explicit checklist is more reliable than a single expert because it replaces stochastic intuitive leaps with a deterministic verification chain, reducing 'omission errors' (forgetting a step) which are the primary failure mode of expert intuition.","counterconditions":["The task requires 'synthesis' rather than 'verification' (i.e., the answer cannot be decomposed into checklist items).","The checklist is so comprehensive that it triggers the 'Checklist Paradox' (SCR_004), where bureaucratic compliance replaces active verification."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist acts as an external cognitive scaffold that enforces a complete search of the problem space. While the expert uses 'compressed expertise' (SRC_003) to jump to a conclusion, the panel's reliability is derived from the additive probability of multiple agents detecting a single checklist violation, effectively transforming a high-variance individual process into a low-variance group process.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["e55c3de6477bd8f3ad420cd44cb871ccd799098c2a71b8d427415fd30759468c","88e83df284a970d327702560c656f58dd7f1b0badf69c97cb47feb0ba6ad3d93"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The single domain expert remains more reliable because genuine reliability in complex tasks depends on 'pattern recognition of anomalies' that cannot be codified in a checklist without the checklist becoming an exhaustive (and thus unusable) manual.","counterconditions":["The task is highly standardized with a finite set of failure modes.","The expert is operating under high cognitive load or fatigue, increasing the rate of omission errors."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is a form of high-dimensional pattern matching. A checklist, by definition, is a low-dimensional projection of that expertise. The reliability gain of the panel is illusory (SRC_004) because it only tracks 'known knowns,' whereas the expert's reliability stems from the ability to detect 'unknown unknowns' (SRC_008) that fall between the lines of the checklist items.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["d61cc68c8fb9c612a7a7a0ae1bf95d9568bb41e30ffc5a40f152cd1c75ee5885","88e83df284a970d327702560c656f58dd7f1b0badf69c97cb47feb0ba6ad3d93"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere compliance by the presence of 'cognitive friction'—specifically, a non-uniform distribution of time and effort spent per checklist item that correlates with the item's objective difficulty.","counterconditions":["The panel is incentivized by speed/KPIs, forcing them to simulate friction or hide it.","The checklist items are designed such that their objective difficulty is not distinguishable to the evaluator."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A compliance-driven panel treats every checkbox as a binary administrative task (constant time). A reliability-driven panel engages in active verification, spending disproportionately more time on 'hard' items. Therefore, the evidence of reliability is the 'time-spike' (SCR_001) on difficult items, proving the panel is actually processing the information rather than just tracking the checklist.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["4c982bb24af1e5ba9b8b2efaf3ffbe6d731eb9e0c880009b2cd6a1899fda908e"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel is more reliable only for 'boundary-case' tasks, and the evidence of genuine gain is a 'negative correlation' between the ease of checklist completion and the correctness of the result in complex cases.","counterconditions":["The checklist is too simplistic to capture the boundary conditions.","The 'struggle' of the panel is due to poor training rather than task complexity."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In simple cases, both expert and panel succeed. In boundary cases, experts often over-rely on 'average-case' intuition (SRC_005). The panel's reliability is evidenced when they struggle (high friction, SCR_003) and still arrive at the correct answer, whereas a compliance-driven panel would find it 'easy' to check the boxes and potentially arrive at a wrong answer because they missed the nuance of the boundary case.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["4e41de1c8bcad969cfd68e5841eaeafacd2b61ebdd275fa97e90352502ea6488"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Reliability gain is evidenced by 'error-type symmetry': the panel is more reliable if it shifts the failure mode from 'omission' (expert forgetting a step) to 'commission' (panel misinterpreting a step), provided commission errors are more easily detectable via audit.","counterconditions":["Commission errors are more catastrophic than omission errors in the specific domain.","The audit process for checklist commission errors is as unreliable as the expert's intuition."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist eliminates the 'forgotten step' (omission). While the panel may still fail by misapplying a rule (commission), this failure leaves a legible trail in the checklist (SCR_002). The 'reliability' gain is not necessarily a higher raw accuracy, but a reduction in the variance of the error type, making the system as a whole more stable and auditable.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["e55c3de6477bd8f3ad420cd44cb871ccd799098c2a71b8d427415fd30759468c","88e83df284a970d327702560c656f58dd7f1b0badf69c97cb47feb0ba6ad3d93","d61cc68c8fb9c612a7a7a0ae1bf95d9568bb41e30ffc5a40f152cd1c75ee5885","4c982bb24af1e5ba9b8b2efaf3ffbe6d731eb9e0c880009b2cd6a1899fda908e","4e41de1c8bcad969cfd68e5841eaeafacd2b61ebdd275fa97e90352502ea6488"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Consider the 'Inter-Panel Variance' as a diagnostic. If we have multiple panels using the same checklist, mere compliance would lead to high agreement (everyone checks the boxes). Genuine reliability would lead to variance on difficult items but convergence on the correct answer. If agreement is 100% but the answer is wrong, it's a compliance failure. If agreement is low but the majority is correct, it's a reliability process at work.","why_keep_this":"Proposes a group-level metric to distinguish compliance from reliability."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is there a threshold of 'training' where a non-expert becomes an 'expert' but loses the discipline of the checklist, effectively merging the two failure modes?","related_refs":["SCR_007"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}